WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.740 --> 00:00:07.420
TOM SALMON: Welcome
to our session.

00:00:07.420 --> 00:00:11.230
This is a joint session between
Google Cloud and Refinitiv,

00:00:11.230 --> 00:00:14.750
one of the leading providers
of financial market data.

00:00:14.750 --> 00:00:16.400
This session is a
great case study

00:00:16.400 --> 00:00:21.140
of how BigQuery brings the speed
and scale of cloud computing

00:00:21.140 --> 00:00:23.460
to financial market data.

00:00:23.460 --> 00:00:25.710
We hear from
customers, the headache

00:00:25.710 --> 00:00:28.050
of managing an on
premise infrastructure

00:00:28.050 --> 00:00:31.110
to provide fast access to
petabytes worth of data

00:00:31.110 --> 00:00:33.400
is a significant challenge.

00:00:33.400 --> 00:00:35.320
This is a case
study of how we work

00:00:35.320 --> 00:00:38.440
with Refinitiv to deliver
analysis-ready financial data

00:00:38.440 --> 00:00:40.570
at scale.

00:00:40.570 --> 00:00:42.910
My name is Tom Salmon
and I've been with Google

00:00:42.910 --> 00:00:45.070
for around three and
a half years in the UK

00:00:45.070 --> 00:00:47.200
financial services business.

00:00:47.200 --> 00:00:49.690
I've been working deeply with
Refinitiv for the last 18

00:00:49.690 --> 00:00:52.390
months to help them take
advantage of Google Cloud

00:00:52.390 --> 00:00:55.560
and bring advantage to
our mutual customers.

00:00:55.560 --> 00:00:58.165
I'm going to hand
over now to Catalina.

00:00:58.165 --> 00:00:59.540
CATALINA VAZQUEZ:
Thank you, Tom.

00:00:59.540 --> 00:01:01.020
Great being here today.

00:01:01.020 --> 00:01:02.420
My name is Catalina Vasquez.

00:01:02.420 --> 00:01:05.102
I head up the historical
database inside Refinitiv.

00:01:05.102 --> 00:01:06.560
We're constantly
looking at solving

00:01:06.560 --> 00:01:09.110
some of the most difficult
problems our users face when

00:01:09.110 --> 00:01:12.530
accessing new data sets as well
as onboarding new sources that

00:01:12.530 --> 00:01:15.330
will be relevant for workflows
across the front, middle,

00:01:15.330 --> 00:01:16.430
and back office.

00:01:16.430 --> 00:01:18.620
We have been working
with Google Cloud

00:01:18.620 --> 00:01:21.020
as they've provided a
unique opportunity to solve

00:01:21.020 --> 00:01:23.480
one of the largest
challenges our customers face

00:01:23.480 --> 00:01:27.520
when dealing with large amounts
of historical pricing data.

00:01:27.520 --> 00:01:31.120
Refinitiv is one of the largest
providers of financial markets

00:01:31.120 --> 00:01:32.800
data and infrastructure.

00:01:32.800 --> 00:01:36.580
We serve over 40,000
institutions and approximately

00:01:36.580 --> 00:01:39.370
operate in 190 countries.

00:01:39.370 --> 00:01:43.270
We provide leading data and
insights, trading platforms,

00:01:43.270 --> 00:01:45.370
and open data and
technology platforms

00:01:45.370 --> 00:01:47.200
that connect the global
financial markets

00:01:47.200 --> 00:01:49.300
community in different ways.

00:01:49.300 --> 00:01:52.760
We help drive performance in
trading, investment management,

00:01:52.760 --> 00:01:55.870
wealth management, regulatory
compliance, market data

00:01:55.870 --> 00:01:59.290
management, and also
fighting financial crime.

00:01:59.290 --> 00:02:01.690
Our tech history
offering sits at the core

00:02:01.690 --> 00:02:05.800
of key workflows for quants,
data scientists, traders,

00:02:05.800 --> 00:02:08.110
and compliance functions.

00:02:08.110 --> 00:02:11.208
In this context, let's
pause for a second and open

00:02:11.208 --> 00:02:13.000
up the conversation
with one of the largest

00:02:13.000 --> 00:02:14.740
challenges our
customers face when it

00:02:14.740 --> 00:02:17.080
comes to financial market data.

00:02:17.080 --> 00:02:19.480
For every dollar spent
on financial market data,

00:02:19.480 --> 00:02:22.900
a further eight are spent
in processing, storing,

00:02:22.900 --> 00:02:26.290
and transforming that data
before it can be analyzed.

00:02:26.290 --> 00:02:28.990
So wouldn't it be great if
we could help change this?

00:02:28.990 --> 00:02:32.020
We saw a great opportunity to
work together with Google Cloud

00:02:32.020 --> 00:02:34.962
to solve this problem.

00:02:34.962 --> 00:02:36.420
From the very
beginning, we knew we

00:02:36.420 --> 00:02:38.045
were dealing with a
data set that would

00:02:38.045 --> 00:02:40.420
be petabytes worth of data.

00:02:40.420 --> 00:02:43.260
So I'll take you down a
short stroll down memory lane

00:02:43.260 --> 00:02:45.580
to understand exactly
what tech history is

00:02:45.580 --> 00:02:48.600
and how the history of
tech history started.

00:02:48.600 --> 00:02:51.000
On the left, you will see
that we're back in the year

00:02:51.000 --> 00:02:54.600
1867, when the first stock
ticker was unveiled in New York

00:02:54.600 --> 00:02:55.680
City.

00:02:55.680 --> 00:02:57.510
This revolutionized
the stock market

00:02:57.510 --> 00:03:00.780
by making prices available to
investors around the country.

00:03:00.780 --> 00:03:04.200
Before that, stock
tickers were actually

00:03:04.200 --> 00:03:06.760
traveling by mail or messenger.

00:03:06.760 --> 00:03:09.790
After a number of iterations,
the celebrated Thomas Edison,

00:03:09.790 --> 00:03:13.060
amongst a few other inventors,
patented an improved version

00:03:13.060 --> 00:03:14.320
of the stock ticker.

00:03:14.320 --> 00:03:16.450
It was, indeed, one of
the first inventions

00:03:16.450 --> 00:03:18.490
he managed to monetize.

00:03:18.490 --> 00:03:20.140
The stock ticker
was later replaced

00:03:20.140 --> 00:03:22.900
by a computerized ticker
with electronic displays

00:03:22.900 --> 00:03:24.940
back in the 1960s.

00:03:24.940 --> 00:03:27.580
We now fast forward
to 1969, and we

00:03:27.580 --> 00:03:31.870
can see that ticker tape is all
across the streets of Chicago.

00:03:31.870 --> 00:03:36.072
People are welcoming Apollo 11
astronauts back in the city.

00:03:36.072 --> 00:03:37.780
Ticker tape was,
therefore, not only just

00:03:37.780 --> 00:03:39.790
used in financial
markets, but also

00:03:39.790 --> 00:03:43.490
to celebrate important
milestones across the world.

00:03:43.490 --> 00:03:47.230
And finally, our time machine
takes us to the year 1997,

00:03:47.230 --> 00:03:48.850
to a pub in London.

00:03:48.850 --> 00:03:50.620
While internet
access was booming,

00:03:50.620 --> 00:03:52.120
there was growing
interest to look

00:03:52.120 --> 00:03:54.370
at quantitative
analysis for trading,

00:03:54.370 --> 00:03:57.100
as well as process automation
happening in the trading

00:03:57.100 --> 00:03:58.360
workflows.

00:03:58.360 --> 00:04:01.510
Algorithmic trading required
increasing volumes of data

00:04:01.510 --> 00:04:04.270
to create and test
these strategies.

00:04:04.270 --> 00:04:07.150
A group of academics
finds out that we

00:04:07.150 --> 00:04:09.730
have been storing ticker
tapes in our vaults for years

00:04:09.730 --> 00:04:12.370
and wanted to access this
archive of every stock market

00:04:12.370 --> 00:04:14.590
transaction to
conduct some research,

00:04:14.590 --> 00:04:17.750
looking at market
microstructure and behavior.

00:04:17.750 --> 00:04:19.990
This is how tech
history was then born.

00:04:19.990 --> 00:04:22.720
Refinitiv collects
the data, arriving

00:04:22.720 --> 00:04:24.580
from markets and
sources around the world

00:04:24.580 --> 00:04:26.260
in unstructured formats.

00:04:26.260 --> 00:04:29.200
We normalize, standardize,
and enrich this content

00:04:29.200 --> 00:04:30.890
to make it usable.

00:04:30.890 --> 00:04:33.700
So you can distinguish between
a government bond and a spot

00:04:33.700 --> 00:04:36.610
effects rate, despite the fact
that every market and data

00:04:36.610 --> 00:04:38.680
source uses different
conventions,

00:04:38.680 --> 00:04:41.260
and different methods,
and describing factors

00:04:41.260 --> 00:04:44.230
like instrument codes,
timestamps, location,

00:04:44.230 --> 00:04:45.790
or currency codes.

00:04:45.790 --> 00:04:47.870
As an end user, you
can consume the data

00:04:47.870 --> 00:04:51.950
set in one normalized
and structured format.

00:04:51.950 --> 00:04:55.430
Refinitiv tech history carries
over 20 years of pricing data

00:04:55.430 --> 00:04:59.180
from over 500 exchanges
and specialist data sets.

00:04:59.180 --> 00:05:02.390
This archive grows daily,
at a significant rate.

00:05:02.390 --> 00:05:04.670
It's over five
petabytes worth of data,

00:05:04.670 --> 00:05:08.450
compressed and archived,
ready for you to consume.

00:05:08.450 --> 00:05:10.742
This requires
significant resources,

00:05:10.742 --> 00:05:12.200
both to ensure that
you can keep up

00:05:12.200 --> 00:05:14.780
with the rate at which new
data points are generated,

00:05:14.780 --> 00:05:16.400
but also to make
sure that you have

00:05:16.400 --> 00:05:18.350
the right infrastructure
required to deal

00:05:18.350 --> 00:05:20.870
with such large data volumes.

00:05:20.870 --> 00:05:23.630
Think of the compute, the
storage, and the network

00:05:23.630 --> 00:05:26.690
costs linked to
doing this in house.

00:05:26.690 --> 00:05:29.530
So going back to our
initial problem statement.

00:05:29.530 --> 00:05:32.120
For every dollar
spent in market data,

00:05:32.120 --> 00:05:34.480
customers are spending
an additional eight,

00:05:34.480 --> 00:05:38.590
without even getting the
data ready to be analyzed.

00:05:38.590 --> 00:05:40.270
We saw in Google
Cloud, an opportunity

00:05:40.270 --> 00:05:42.940
to tackle the largest challenge
for historical data, which

00:05:42.940 --> 00:05:46.970
is actually the ETL process,
or, Extract, Transform,

00:05:46.970 --> 00:05:48.520
and Load process.

00:05:48.520 --> 00:05:51.550
Traditionally, this would be
the customer's responsibility,

00:05:51.550 --> 00:05:54.640
which meant each customer has
to keep a copy of the data set

00:05:54.640 --> 00:05:58.210
in-house and had to allocate
expensive resources to unlock

00:05:58.210 --> 00:05:59.550
access to it.

00:05:59.550 --> 00:06:01.930
With BigQuery, we
have the opportunity

00:06:01.930 --> 00:06:05.110
to transform our offering
and move away from a download

00:06:05.110 --> 00:06:08.830
service, to now being able to
offer a managed tech history

00:06:08.830 --> 00:06:12.550
database, allowing our
end users to access

00:06:12.550 --> 00:06:15.220
that single source of
truth, ready for query,

00:06:15.220 --> 00:06:16.940
as and when you need it.

00:06:16.940 --> 00:06:19.130
So we stopped moving
the data around

00:06:19.130 --> 00:06:21.850
and provided access to the data
where the capability actually

00:06:21.850 --> 00:06:22.720
is.

00:06:22.720 --> 00:06:27.220
This drastically reduces
timelines to access the data,

00:06:27.220 --> 00:06:29.650
reduces the complexity
in the process,

00:06:29.650 --> 00:06:32.350
but also ensures that a
single source of truth

00:06:32.350 --> 00:06:35.530
is accessible to unlock
the right opportunities

00:06:35.530 --> 00:06:38.290
across financial services.

00:06:38.290 --> 00:06:41.500
So would you rather have your
quants and data scientists

00:06:41.500 --> 00:06:44.290
adding value to the business,
or cleaning, aggregating,

00:06:44.290 --> 00:06:46.390
and waiting for data access?

00:06:46.390 --> 00:06:48.280
The reality is that
most of the time

00:06:48.280 --> 00:06:50.590
would traditionally be spent
on cleaning, processing,

00:06:50.590 --> 00:06:52.240
and transforming the data.

00:06:52.240 --> 00:06:55.330
But we can now shift
this 80-20 rule around.

00:06:55.330 --> 00:06:58.670
We can ensure that the
business gets what they want.

00:06:58.670 --> 00:07:01.780
We can maximize the time
that data scientists, quants,

00:07:01.780 --> 00:07:04.720
and end users spend on
adding value to the business

00:07:04.720 --> 00:07:07.150
by providing timely access
to the data they need,

00:07:07.150 --> 00:07:10.390
where they use it to run
machine learning models at scale

00:07:10.390 --> 00:07:13.510
or build and backtest
new trading strategies.

00:07:13.510 --> 00:07:15.580
We're already
seeing our customers

00:07:15.580 --> 00:07:17.510
very happy about this.

00:07:17.510 --> 00:07:20.650
We're able to enable
new workflows and also

00:07:20.650 --> 00:07:22.630
new businesses to succeed.

00:07:22.630 --> 00:07:24.160
We're allowing them
to focus on what

00:07:24.160 --> 00:07:26.770
truly allows them to
differentiate in a highly

00:07:26.770 --> 00:07:29.680
competitive environment.

00:07:29.680 --> 00:07:32.760
So a single source of truth
that tech history provides

00:07:32.760 --> 00:07:35.430
is used across the
full trading lifecycle.

00:07:35.430 --> 00:07:37.290
It enables a more
efficient allocation

00:07:37.290 --> 00:07:40.080
of resources across
the organization,

00:07:40.080 --> 00:07:42.720
from preventing quants
and data scientists

00:07:42.720 --> 00:07:45.120
from sitting idle,
waiting for data access,

00:07:45.120 --> 00:07:47.580
to unnecessary
infrastructure spend.

00:07:47.580 --> 00:07:50.220
From the pre-trade activities
that quants and data scientists

00:07:50.220 --> 00:07:53.670
would perform for alpha seeking,
such as designing and back

00:07:53.670 --> 00:07:56.850
testing new trading strategies,
to the at and post trade

00:07:56.850 --> 00:08:00.180
analysis that would look to
reduce slippage or recreate

00:08:00.180 --> 00:08:01.740
order book analysis.

00:08:01.740 --> 00:08:04.260
And then finally, to
fulfilling compliance

00:08:04.260 --> 00:08:06.900
and regulatory requirements
around transaction

00:08:06.900 --> 00:08:10.560
cost analysis or algo
conformance testing.

00:08:10.560 --> 00:08:13.140
One of the areas where we
find significant interest

00:08:13.140 --> 00:08:15.270
in leveraging tech
history and BigQuery,

00:08:15.270 --> 00:08:18.080
is within the quant and
data scientist community.

00:08:18.080 --> 00:08:20.700
Tom is now going to cover
in a bit more detail, how

00:08:20.700 --> 00:08:23.250
we can enable focus
on alpha generation,

00:08:23.250 --> 00:08:25.200
rather than managing
IT infrastructure.

00:08:25.200 --> 00:08:26.717
So Tom, over to you.

00:08:26.717 --> 00:08:28.800
TOM SALMON: So I'd like
to dig a little bit deeper

00:08:28.800 --> 00:08:32.280
into the life of a quant
using Google Cloud.

00:08:32.280 --> 00:08:36.299
I use quant here interchangeably
for data scientists,

00:08:36.299 --> 00:08:39.900
data engineers, and
even traders that code.

00:08:39.900 --> 00:08:43.750
Really, it's anyone that wants
to get insight from data.

00:08:43.750 --> 00:08:46.210
So I've broken this down
into four different pillars.

00:08:46.210 --> 00:08:48.930
We have collecting
and transforming data,

00:08:48.930 --> 00:08:51.960
finding patterns, training
models, and then deploying

00:08:51.960 --> 00:08:53.690
those models.

00:08:53.690 --> 00:08:56.210
Collecting and
transforming that data

00:08:56.210 --> 00:08:58.320
has three different categories.

00:08:58.320 --> 00:09:00.560
The streaming data,
which might be

00:09:00.560 --> 00:09:02.600
up to the second,
real time stock market

00:09:02.600 --> 00:09:04.220
data that's coming in.

00:09:04.220 --> 00:09:07.550
You might collect that
through services like Pub/Sub.

00:09:07.550 --> 00:09:09.440
You might be collecting
it and transforming

00:09:09.440 --> 00:09:11.330
it using data flowing.

00:09:11.330 --> 00:09:14.860
You could also stream it
directly into BigQuery as well.

00:09:14.860 --> 00:09:18.140
BigQuery, here we're talking
around BigQuery for storage,

00:09:18.140 --> 00:09:20.320
designed for exabytes
worth of storage,

00:09:20.320 --> 00:09:23.330
which we'll talk about
in more detail later on.

00:09:23.330 --> 00:09:25.410
In this example,
Refinitiv tech history

00:09:25.410 --> 00:09:27.710
is loaded and immediately
available for querying

00:09:27.710 --> 00:09:29.340
in BigQuery.

00:09:29.340 --> 00:09:32.650
To complement this, firms
will bring their own data.

00:09:32.650 --> 00:09:35.610
Position data, historical
trading data, and maybe

00:09:35.610 --> 00:09:37.610
other data sets that they
have available to them

00:09:37.610 --> 00:09:40.957
or that they've curated
and generated in-house.

00:09:40.957 --> 00:09:42.540
Where there isn't a
structure to data,

00:09:42.540 --> 00:09:44.930
we consider that
alternative data.

00:09:44.930 --> 00:09:47.040
You'd load that into
Google Cloud Storage,

00:09:47.040 --> 00:09:48.930
which is an object storage.

00:09:48.930 --> 00:09:50.550
And from here,
typically, you want

00:09:50.550 --> 00:09:53.700
to find patterns and
quantize that data.

00:09:53.700 --> 00:09:56.520
They would use Google
Cloud's ML services,

00:09:56.520 --> 00:09:59.310
for example, the natural
language processing API.

00:09:59.310 --> 00:10:01.590
You can give it a PDF
document and extract

00:10:01.590 --> 00:10:05.500
the key terms, and phrases,
and numbers from that document.

00:10:05.500 --> 00:10:07.830
You could also take
an image and process

00:10:07.830 --> 00:10:10.590
that image to understand
the contents of it

00:10:10.590 --> 00:10:13.480
and to understand what
it's actually looking at.

00:10:13.480 --> 00:10:15.880
Once you have these different
data sets that are stored

00:10:15.880 --> 00:10:17.297
and you've done
the transformation

00:10:17.297 --> 00:10:18.910
that you need to
make it available,

00:10:18.910 --> 00:10:22.490
we then move to the analysis
phase to find those patterns.

00:10:22.490 --> 00:10:26.440
So I've outlined three key
tools that people typically use.

00:10:26.440 --> 00:10:28.500
At the top, we have Looker.

00:10:28.500 --> 00:10:31.300
Looker is our
visualization platform

00:10:31.300 --> 00:10:33.820
for looking at very
large amounts of data

00:10:33.820 --> 00:10:37.180
and finding interesting
patterns through charts.

00:10:37.180 --> 00:10:40.150
People might build
services like heat maps.

00:10:40.150 --> 00:10:42.190
They might be using this
for time series data

00:10:42.190 --> 00:10:45.580
to understand the relationship
between different data sets.

00:10:45.580 --> 00:10:48.610
To get deeper, you'll be moving
into BigQuery for the analysis

00:10:48.610 --> 00:10:53.490
phase, writing secret queries
for advanced analytics.

00:10:53.490 --> 00:10:56.490
Alongside this, you can also
build machine learning models

00:10:56.490 --> 00:10:59.160
directly inside BigQuery.

00:10:59.160 --> 00:11:04.330
BigQuery ML extends SQL syntax
with an ML specific syntax,

00:11:04.330 --> 00:11:07.017
allowing you to train
models directly in BigQuery,

00:11:07.017 --> 00:11:08.850
taking advantage of
it's architecture, which

00:11:08.850 --> 00:11:11.910
I'll explain more
deeply in future slides.

00:11:11.910 --> 00:11:15.570
Thirdly, we then have users
that want to write code,

00:11:15.570 --> 00:11:18.210
data scientists that want
to use Jupyter notebooks

00:11:18.210 --> 00:11:21.870
or people that want to write
TensorFlow models directly.

00:11:21.870 --> 00:11:25.830
They can spin up Jupyter
notebooks using Cloud AI Hub.

00:11:25.830 --> 00:11:29.310
And then Cloud AI Hub has direct
connections and connectivity

00:11:29.310 --> 00:11:32.520
into BigQuery, into
Google Cloud Storage.

00:11:32.520 --> 00:11:35.653
The key is, this is a platform
that's horizontally integrated.

00:11:35.653 --> 00:11:38.070
You're not having to deal with
networking and connectivity

00:11:38.070 --> 00:11:39.470
and firewall rules.

00:11:39.470 --> 00:11:43.650
All services have native
connectivity to each other.

00:11:43.650 --> 00:11:45.740
So now we've found some
interesting pattern

00:11:45.740 --> 00:11:47.360
or hypothesis.

00:11:47.360 --> 00:11:49.880
We want to go and train
a model and backtest

00:11:49.880 --> 00:11:52.250
and validate what it looks like.

00:11:52.250 --> 00:11:54.440
So we'll then be moving
to our third pillar, which

00:11:54.440 --> 00:11:56.510
is training models.

00:11:56.510 --> 00:11:58.880
So training those
models, underlying it

00:11:58.880 --> 00:12:00.710
needs a large amount of compute.

00:12:00.710 --> 00:12:03.530
And we have different
flavors available.

00:12:03.530 --> 00:12:08.360
For CPUs, you can access Intel
Skylake CPUs and Cascade Lake.

00:12:08.360 --> 00:12:11.020
And even newer variants are
always coming available.

00:12:11.020 --> 00:12:14.180
And these are in global data
centers around the world.

00:12:14.180 --> 00:12:16.680
For machine learning
workloads, commonly, people

00:12:16.680 --> 00:12:19.880
will use GPUs, Graphical
Processing Units, because they

00:12:19.880 --> 00:12:22.880
significantly increase
the power available

00:12:22.880 --> 00:12:26.680
and reduce the training time
for machine learning models.

00:12:26.680 --> 00:12:29.480
Totally unique to Google,
we have a third level

00:12:29.480 --> 00:12:32.300
called TPUs, Tensor
Processing Units,

00:12:32.300 --> 00:12:34.270
which I've pictured here.

00:12:34.270 --> 00:12:37.300
TPUs were designed
and built by Google

00:12:37.300 --> 00:12:40.600
using custom silicon for our
precise needs for machine

00:12:40.600 --> 00:12:42.080
learning.

00:12:42.080 --> 00:12:45.110
To improve the accuracy
of Google search results,

00:12:45.110 --> 00:12:48.140
to write better recommendations
for YouTube videos,

00:12:48.140 --> 00:12:51.140
we needed to significantly
increase, through a step

00:12:51.140 --> 00:12:55.400
change, the price performance
and power performance,

00:12:55.400 --> 00:12:58.350
beyond GPUs, to a new level.

00:12:58.350 --> 00:13:00.710
TPUs gave us that ability.

00:13:00.710 --> 00:13:02.820
And you can rent,
effectively, some

00:13:02.820 --> 00:13:05.490
of the most powerful
supercomputers in the world

00:13:05.490 --> 00:13:08.230
by accessing TPUs.

00:13:08.230 --> 00:13:09.880
So now you've
trained your model.

00:13:09.880 --> 00:13:12.250
You've done your back
testing and your validation.

00:13:12.250 --> 00:13:15.460
You then need to deploy
it to make it useful.

00:13:15.460 --> 00:13:18.240
Cloud ML Engine allows you to
take the models that you've

00:13:18.240 --> 00:13:21.610
trained through HPC computing--

00:13:21.610 --> 00:13:23.500
You might have trained
them with TPUs.

00:13:23.500 --> 00:13:25.810
You might have trained
them inside BigQuery.

00:13:25.810 --> 00:13:28.110
You can then turn
them into an API.

00:13:28.110 --> 00:13:30.580
You can turn it into
a RESTful endpoint.

00:13:30.580 --> 00:13:32.320
And it also has
built-in batch features,

00:13:32.320 --> 00:13:34.195
so you can run batch
predictions against data

00:13:34.195 --> 00:13:37.503
that's in BigQuery or
inside Google Cloud Storage.

00:13:37.503 --> 00:13:39.670
And now we're going to go
a bit deeper into BigQuery

00:13:39.670 --> 00:13:41.970
to tell you about the
advantages of why BigQuery

00:13:41.970 --> 00:13:44.110
is the best platform for this.

00:13:44.110 --> 00:13:46.360
I'm going to focus slightly
deeper into BigQuery

00:13:46.360 --> 00:13:49.060
now because I feel it's
really important to underline

00:13:49.060 --> 00:13:51.470
the capabilities and
what makes it different.

00:13:51.470 --> 00:13:55.150
The key headline for
BigQuery is, focus on insight

00:13:55.150 --> 00:13:57.280
not infrastructure.

00:13:57.280 --> 00:14:00.300
Google realized over the
years, that when analysts

00:14:00.300 --> 00:14:02.790
have to think about the
size of the data warehouse

00:14:02.790 --> 00:14:04.890
and the amount of
capacity, they end up

00:14:04.890 --> 00:14:07.410
writing queries limited
by the infrastructure,

00:14:07.410 --> 00:14:09.970
not limited by
their imagination.

00:14:09.970 --> 00:14:13.050
So what we do, and the key
innovation of BigQuery,

00:14:13.050 --> 00:14:15.480
is it's a serverless platform.

00:14:15.480 --> 00:14:18.240
Google takes care of the
scaling, the operations,

00:14:18.240 --> 00:14:20.100
and the resiliency.

00:14:20.100 --> 00:14:23.490
It's a platform that you can
load in exabytes worth of data

00:14:23.490 --> 00:14:26.400
and run petabyte-scale queries
over hundreds of trillions

00:14:26.400 --> 00:14:29.670
of rows, and we automatically
scale it and manage it

00:14:29.670 --> 00:14:31.358
on your behalf.

00:14:31.358 --> 00:14:32.900
This means that when
your quants want

00:14:32.900 --> 00:14:34.760
to find interesting
patterns, they

00:14:34.760 --> 00:14:37.295
don't have to make trade-offs
as to how much compute

00:14:37.295 --> 00:14:39.140
is available and
how many servers

00:14:39.140 --> 00:14:41.090
are being used right now.

00:14:41.090 --> 00:14:43.520
A key problem that we
hear from customers

00:14:43.520 --> 00:14:46.093
is, the team in London
is OK during the morning,

00:14:46.093 --> 00:14:48.260
but then it comes to lunchtime
and the New York team

00:14:48.260 --> 00:14:51.050
come online, and suddenly
they get more competitive

00:14:51.050 --> 00:14:52.740
towards the data warehouse.

00:14:52.740 --> 00:14:55.790
There's a fixed number of calls
or a fixed amount of disk.

00:14:55.790 --> 00:14:58.190
And then San Francisco comes
online and then further

00:14:58.190 --> 00:14:59.328
countries.

00:14:59.328 --> 00:15:01.370
And suddenly there's too
many [INAUDIBLE] people,

00:15:01.370 --> 00:15:04.070
computing for too few
resources, and you end up

00:15:04.070 --> 00:15:06.890
with contention and
everything slows down.

00:15:06.890 --> 00:15:08.750
And then you revert
back to the focus,

00:15:08.750 --> 00:15:10.980
is writing queries that
fit the infrastructure,

00:15:10.980 --> 00:15:14.410
not queries that add
value to your business.

00:15:14.410 --> 00:15:17.440
Underlying all of this, is
the architectural difference

00:15:17.440 --> 00:15:18.640
around BigQuery.

00:15:18.640 --> 00:15:20.560
We released a white
paper on the technology

00:15:20.560 --> 00:15:24.730
we call Dremel, inside Google,
that we developed 10 years ago.

00:15:24.730 --> 00:15:27.720
There's two key
components of Dremel.

00:15:27.720 --> 00:15:30.210
On the left-hand
side is storage.

00:15:30.210 --> 00:15:34.890
Replicated, distributed storage
with 11 nines of durability.

00:15:34.890 --> 00:15:36.990
We can store exabytes of data.

00:15:36.990 --> 00:15:41.460
100% of that is hot, online,
active, and queryable.

00:15:41.460 --> 00:15:43.860
It has the same latency
profile, whether you're

00:15:43.860 --> 00:15:47.250
querying data for an hour
ago, or 15 years ago.

00:15:47.250 --> 00:15:50.130
It's immediately
available for querying.

00:15:50.130 --> 00:15:53.880
The right-hand side is a highly
available compute cluster

00:15:53.880 --> 00:15:56.940
that we manage on
behalf of our customers.

00:15:56.940 --> 00:15:59.300
We make available a very
large amount of compute

00:15:59.300 --> 00:16:01.870
in multiple data centers
around the world that

00:16:01.870 --> 00:16:05.040
can be span up, on demand,
for each particular query

00:16:05.040 --> 00:16:06.930
that comes in.

00:16:06.930 --> 00:16:08.850
In the middle,
linking this together,

00:16:08.850 --> 00:16:12.240
is a petabyte-scale
distributed memory tier.

00:16:12.240 --> 00:16:14.250
Very customized, low
latency networking

00:16:14.250 --> 00:16:16.530
to link these nodes together.

00:16:16.530 --> 00:16:19.620
What happens here is,
when a query comes in,

00:16:19.620 --> 00:16:23.370
we analyze that query to
work out the exact right size

00:16:23.370 --> 00:16:26.340
and shape of data warehouse
needed for that specific query

00:16:26.340 --> 00:16:28.410
at that specific time.

00:16:28.410 --> 00:16:30.540
We know exactly
where the data is,

00:16:30.540 --> 00:16:33.480
how we sliced it, sharded it,
partitioned it, and indexed it

00:16:33.480 --> 00:16:34.930
on your behalf.

00:16:34.930 --> 00:16:37.020
So we know how many
machines we might need

00:16:37.020 --> 00:16:39.420
to read that data in parallel.

00:16:39.420 --> 00:16:40.380
So we'll take a guess.

00:16:40.380 --> 00:16:42.860
Maybe it's 100 or
1,000 to read the data.

00:16:42.860 --> 00:16:44.610
Then we'll go through
the different phases

00:16:44.610 --> 00:16:47.280
in the query lifecycle,
doing grouping, aggregation,

00:16:47.280 --> 00:16:50.040
and filtering, and
more dynamically change

00:16:50.040 --> 00:16:52.200
the size of that
cluster for you to use

00:16:52.200 --> 00:16:54.810
the right amount of resources
to return the query as

00:16:54.810 --> 00:16:56.820
quickly as possible.

00:16:56.820 --> 00:16:59.930
So this is automatic and this
is managed on your behalf.

00:16:59.930 --> 00:17:02.930
And every single user
for every single query

00:17:02.930 --> 00:17:05.750
gets the experience
as if a data warehouse

00:17:05.750 --> 00:17:09.619
had been custom built for that
exact query every single time.

00:17:09.619 --> 00:17:13.675
And we spin this up
virtually on the fly for you.

00:17:13.675 --> 00:17:16.050
I'd like to talk briefly around
the shared responsibility

00:17:16.050 --> 00:17:18.869
model, where Google
Cloud provides

00:17:18.869 --> 00:17:21.450
a benefit to Refinitiv,
who provides a benefit

00:17:21.450 --> 00:17:23.050
to our mutual customers.

00:17:23.050 --> 00:17:25.980
Google Cloud is externalizing
the infrastructure and data

00:17:25.980 --> 00:17:29.260
centers that Google has spent
billions of dollars building.

00:17:29.260 --> 00:17:32.310
We build and design our
own physical data centers.

00:17:32.310 --> 00:17:34.680
We build and design our
own physical servers

00:17:34.680 --> 00:17:35.920
and networking.

00:17:35.920 --> 00:17:38.400
And this is public and
available in some great research

00:17:38.400 --> 00:17:40.650
out there for you to look into.

00:17:40.650 --> 00:17:42.960
We take care of managing
the physical infrastructure

00:17:42.960 --> 00:17:44.520
on your behalf.

00:17:44.520 --> 00:17:46.050
And typically, we
see organizations

00:17:46.050 --> 00:17:47.920
having two choices.

00:17:47.920 --> 00:17:50.273
They can consume
infrastructure as a service,

00:17:50.273 --> 00:17:52.440
where they have access to
virtual machines in Google

00:17:52.440 --> 00:17:53.670
Cloud.

00:17:53.670 --> 00:17:56.100
They could take a third
party data warehouse platform

00:17:56.100 --> 00:17:58.380
and they could spin
it up themselves.

00:17:58.380 --> 00:18:00.570
But the first question,
they need to ask

00:18:00.570 --> 00:18:03.720
is, how much storage do I
make available for my users?

00:18:03.720 --> 00:18:07.350
How much RAM and compute
do I provide for my users?

00:18:07.350 --> 00:18:10.200
And ultimately, you end
up making a trade-off.

00:18:10.200 --> 00:18:12.780
Do you build a very
large data warehouse

00:18:12.780 --> 00:18:15.750
and size it for peak capacity
but end up running it

00:18:15.750 --> 00:18:18.090
when users aren't
accessing it, or do you

00:18:18.090 --> 00:18:19.890
make it too small,
such that there's

00:18:19.890 --> 00:18:22.770
too much contention and user
fighting over a finite number

00:18:22.770 --> 00:18:24.770
of resources?

00:18:24.770 --> 00:18:28.450
The difference with BigQuery
is, we manage that for you.

00:18:28.450 --> 00:18:30.710
BigQuery is a
serverless platform

00:18:30.710 --> 00:18:32.640
where we run the software,
the infrastructure,

00:18:32.640 --> 00:18:34.130
and the networking.

00:18:34.130 --> 00:18:36.590
We deal with the updates, the
operations, and the backup,

00:18:36.590 --> 00:18:39.080
and everything to
ensure it's highly

00:18:39.080 --> 00:18:41.850
performant for every user.

00:18:41.850 --> 00:18:45.540
Refinitiv have three core
areas of responsibility.

00:18:45.540 --> 00:18:48.360
Data design, that is,
designing the tables

00:18:48.360 --> 00:18:51.300
and the layout of the data
that users interact with.

00:18:51.300 --> 00:18:55.140
Data management, getting
the data into BigQuery,

00:18:55.140 --> 00:18:57.210
ensuring it's up to
date, and making sure

00:18:57.210 --> 00:18:59.670
that it goes through the
proper data lifecycle.

00:18:59.670 --> 00:19:02.610
And then finally, access
control, sharing the right data

00:19:02.610 --> 00:19:04.830
with the right end users.

00:19:04.830 --> 00:19:06.700
The final piece is customers.

00:19:06.700 --> 00:19:09.700
Their responsibility
is analyzing that data,

00:19:09.700 --> 00:19:13.445
getting the value that is their
job to extract from that data.

00:19:13.445 --> 00:19:15.320
So they don't have to
think about networking,

00:19:15.320 --> 00:19:18.310
and cores, and storage
in the ETL process.

00:19:18.310 --> 00:19:20.350
They focus on doing their job.

00:19:20.350 --> 00:19:24.770
I'm going to hand back to
Catalina now to wrap us up.

00:19:24.770 --> 00:19:26.020
CATALINA VAZQUEZ: Thanks, Tom.

00:19:26.020 --> 00:19:28.560
So as we've seen today,
it is possible to access

00:19:28.560 --> 00:19:30.210
query ready data,
without the need

00:19:30.210 --> 00:19:33.820
to store or manage a real time
market data feed yourself.

00:19:33.820 --> 00:19:36.900
You could also leverage BigQuery
compute and Google Cloud

00:19:36.900 --> 00:19:38.940
tools to analyze
the data and not

00:19:38.940 --> 00:19:42.570
have to worry about
provisioning storage or compute.

00:19:42.570 --> 00:19:45.330
And finally, you can also
ensure that you have access

00:19:45.330 --> 00:19:48.750
to an up-to-date, backed up,
and replicated data source,

00:19:48.750 --> 00:19:50.850
without the need for you
to manage those backups

00:19:50.850 --> 00:19:53.200
or disaster recovery instances.

00:19:53.200 --> 00:19:56.190
It's not just a matter of
reducing the additional $8 that

00:19:56.190 --> 00:19:59.280
are spent on processing,
storing, and transforming

00:19:59.280 --> 00:20:01.710
the data, but it's also
about reducing the time

00:20:01.710 --> 00:20:05.340
it takes to enable end users
across your organization

00:20:05.340 --> 00:20:08.280
to access the relevant data
sets and allow them to focus

00:20:08.280 --> 00:20:11.170
on where they can really make
a difference to your business,

00:20:11.170 --> 00:20:14.070
whether that be the success
of the trading strategy,

00:20:14.070 --> 00:20:19.080
managing risk more effectively,
or reducing costs of execution.

00:20:19.080 --> 00:20:22.660
So hope you enjoyed the session
today and from both of us,

00:20:22.660 --> 00:20:25.190
thanks very much for tuning in.

